        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_24580"/>
        <dc:subject>Commerce (History)</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Mediterranean</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Jewish History</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>1000s</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>900s</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Gli ebrei nel commercio mediterraneo nell'alto medioevo (secc. X–XI)</dc:title>
        <dc:date>1997</dc:date>
        <z:language>Italian</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Gli ebrei nel commercio mediterraneo nell'alto medioevo</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>Harvard</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:subject>
            <dcterms:LCC>
               <rdf:value>Berenson HF3750.7 .O75 1997</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:LCC>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:description>Comment: This paper (first presented in a Spoleto conference) sketches the gradual decline of the Jews' role in East-West trade. Their share in the trade between Europe and the Levant decreased, as they could not supply the Muslims with iron and timber available to the Venetians and other Italian merchants. In the eleventh century, Jewish merchants of the Muslim countries were still very active, but rarely visited Christian ports. They had no share in the trade of slaves, timber, iron, grains or African gold. (Ashtor - 1985 - Recent research on Levantine trade)</dc:description>
        <bib:pages>57-98</bib:pages>
    </bib:BookSection>
