        <dc:subject>1400s</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Genoa</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Mediterranean</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Sources</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>1500s</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Political History</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>1200s</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>1300s</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Diplomatic History</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Renaissance History</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Ferrara (Emilia-Romagna)</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Bologna (Emilia-Romagna)</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Notizie e documenti per servire alla storia delle relazioni di Genova con Bologna</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>This is a study and document collection on the relationship of Ligurians in the schools of Bologna. The first teacher from Liguria that will teach in Bologna is the Jacopo di Albenga, an expert in canon law. The number of Ligurian teachers in Bologna grew in the fourteenth century and even more in the fifteenth century. In Bologna they established the Fieschi college, under the legacy of the Genoese Emanuele Fieschi, bishop of Vercelli, who died in 1348, and founded in 1356 by his nephew Papiniano Fieschi. The Genoese students in Bologna formed a large part of this college. In 1444 Genoa assigned the doctors of Bologna a serious legal-political issue. The author then goes on to talk of relations of Genoa to the House of Savoy regarding the road towards Piedmont and France, which the Genoese needed for reasons of trade. The oldest convention in this regard was signed in Asti in 1225. Documents 32 to 73 range from 1450 to the beginning of the seventeenth century.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>1898</dc:date>
        <z:language>Italian, Latin</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Notizie e documenti per servire alla storia delle relazioni di Genova con Bologna</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>University of Toronto</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:subject>
           <dcterms:LCC><rdf:value>DG975 .R7 D47</rdf:value></dcterms:LCC>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:description>A collection of seventy three Latin documents and a long introductory essay on the relations between Genoa and Bologna during the early modern era. There are several references to slaves and servants made by Genose in Bologna throughout.</dc:description>
        <bib:pages>135-224</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
