    <bib:Article rdf:about="#item_6096">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf rdf:resource="urn:issn:1476-5276"/>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Airaldi</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Gabriella</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_82406"/>
        <dc:subject>Genoa</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Mediterranean</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>1100s</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Military History</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Chronicles</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Memoria e memorie di un cavaliere: Caffaro di Genova</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Caffaro di Caschifellone was a member of the oligarchy governing the commune in Genova, but also the very first official writer and witness of the early development of the city. Both in his main text -- the Annales Ianuenses (1099-1166) -- and his other works, the Lyberatio civitatum Orientis and the Ystoria captionis Almarie et Tortuose, Genova emerges as the dominant port of the Western Mediterranean Sea. When Caffaro describes Guglielmo Embriaco, at the same time a warrior, a sailor and a merchant, who took part to both the sieges of Jerusalem and Caesarea, we are allowed to imagine a figure who is not hugely far from the French “epic” Roland: though the very same fact that Guglielmo is a man of the sea and not one from the continental Europe makes him in a certain way a different “thing”. The truth is that in the whole of Caffaro’s works, for the first time during the Middle Ages, it is the Mediterranean Sea, not the continental lands, which stands as the centrepiece: a vision that would be of the greatest importance in the building of European identity.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2003</dc:date>
        <z:language>It</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Memoria e memorie di un cavaliere</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>University of Toronto</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:subject>
           <dcterms:LCC><rdf:value>D157 .C765 ROBARTS</rdf:value></dcterms:LCC>
        </dc:subject>
        <bib:pages>25-39</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
